{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791697324,"nonce":"CAUDp3NWk1CdYQM229O4adOwh50=","title":"Layout animations measured every pass and every read, so a page's knobs flew in as it was built","message":"The wiki's switch knobs, which have a layout animation, flew in from elsewhere when a page opened.\n\nA layout animation compared each native layout with the one before. While a page is built, layout runs several passes in one frame (a paragraph above reflowing as its flow forms, an indicator placing), and a layout is also made for any read of a size, at whatever size the host then has, before a window is attached. Each counted as the place the element started from, so the first frame drew it moving to where it belongs.\n\nFix: layout animations measure at settleMotion, after a frame's passes, and a window's FrameLayout measures only the layouts it draws, having first forgotten what was laid out before it (rebaseMotion). A host that does not use a FrameLayout measures after every compute, as before. tests/native-motion.mjs shifts an element in the first pass of one compute and requires no move; tests/native-frame.mjs requires that neither a read before the first frame nor one between frames is where an element moves from; each fails without its part.","files":null}]}